import{_ as i,P as a,d as n,e as o,y as s,q as r,f as l}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const y=JSON.parse(`{"title":"D.15 Games, Cooperation, Signals, Commitment, and Implementation","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.15 Games, Cooperation, Signals, Commitment, and Implementation","xkChars":0,"xkWords":506,"xkLessons":{"D.15":{"title":"D.15 Games, Cooperation, Signals, Commitment, and Implementation","lead":"A rule that induces truthful reporting does not establish that implementers will execute it correctly or that people omitted from the model bear no costs. The value of game analysis depends on clearly stating the boundaries of actions, information, resources, and evaluation; the actions allowed by a model cannot be treated as all the possibilities in reality.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 5206–5221; source anchor section-d-15; short quotation, translated: 'A mechanism guarantee must specify the set of permitted actions.' The source passage for this topic is also preserved verbatim in source-slice.md; the explanation is separate from the source.","A game representation first specifies who acts when, what they know, what they can actually choose, what outcomes result, and how those outcomes are evaluated. Inclusion in the player set does not confer authority to represent all relevant people; people not listed as players may still bear costs and hold rights. The model's control nodes and the people who ought to be considered normatively are not the same list. Someone's harm cannot be removed from evaluation because they make no active decision.","If a third party can change permissions or resources, it should appear in the relevant causal action model. If it currently has no control node, its standing must still be retained in normative allocation. Omitting an implementer or resource constraint can change equilibrium and implementation outcomes. Once added, the relevant problem must be solved again; merely appending a statement about real-world complexity outside the old model does not permit the old conclusion to cover the new scope.","The existence of an equilibrium, which equilibrium is selected, whether it is actually reached, dynamic stability, efficiency, and justification are six different questions. A Nash equilibrium only indicates that no unilateral deviation is better under the given strategy and belief conditions. It does not guarantee that participants can find it, much less establish that it is most efficient or fairest. Multiple equilibria do not mean that the model automatically selects the actual historical outcome; selection and learning processes require separate explanation.","Repeated interaction may allow future consequences to support cooperation, but it may also undermine cooperation through false reports, short-term incentives, and uncontrolled punishment. Attaching sufficient importance to the future is only a condition in some models; actual relationships also involve monitoring error, exit, remedy, and power. 'Harsher punishment produces more cooperation' cannot be stated as a universal law, nor can a theoretical punishment strategy be treated as permission for real-world implementation.","For a costly signal to discriminate, examine whether incentives differ across types, whether they can bear the cost, and how recipients interpret the signal. If the net benefits for both types induce the same signal, even a high cost does not distinguish them. If a disadvantaged type cannot bear the cost at all, the signal may screen for capacity rather than sincerity. Cost itself is not sufficient evidence of credibility or sincerity.","Commitment has three separate aspects: declaring it, having reasons to fulfill it in the future, and actually being able to fulfill it when the time comes. Saying one will act does not guarantee aligned incentives; willingness to act may coexist with insufficient resources or authority. Deterrence aims to prevent an action, while demanding the reversal of an action already taken is a different task. A self-description as tough is not evidence of effectiveness, and whether a threat ends after the other party complies also affects incentives and credibility. This is an analysis of conditions, not an operational plan for coercing others.","Truthful-reporting guarantees in mechanism design usually apply only to explicitly specified messages, actions, and payoff structures. Truthful reporting does not automatically resolve hidden actions, supervisors' incentives, shared funding, multiple equilibria, or actual implementation authority. The reporting stage may succeed within its original contract while implementation fails separately. Revise the particular node rather than declaring all rational models useless.","The set of lawful actions permitted by rules may differ from the set of actions available in reality. Having no profitable deviation within the lawful set establishes only a conclusion within that restriction. If profitable violations remain possible in reality, the model should include them as possible events in e(t) and analyze their consequences. Including violations in a forecast does not permit their execution; prohibition by a rule does not make them physically impossible. The two sets must be identified separately.","The limited teaching proof for a second-price auction illustrates how conditions do their work. Assume a single item, individual private values, quasilinear payoffs, no collusion, and transactions executed according to the rules, and fix the other bidders' highest bid m. If one's value v exceeds m, the payoff from winning, v−m, is positive, and a truthful bid of v wins; lowering the bid enough to lose merely forgoes this payoff. If v is below m, losing for a payoff of 0 is better than winning for a negative payoff. If they are equal, winning and losing both yield 0. Truthful bidding is therefore weakly dominant; it is not guaranteed to be strictly unique or to maximize seller revenue, and the proof does not cover collusion, refusal to pay, or third-party harms.","The core, Shapley allocations, and bargaining solutions answer different cooperation questions. Whether a coalition can block an allocation, how to allocate by marginal contributions, and how to bargain under given threat points and axioms are different criteria. No name naturally produces a uniquely fair allocation. Normative choices, harmed outsiders, and implementation conditions still need to be made public. Conditions for leaving a game-theoretic explanation should also be specific: if actions, information, or values cannot be represented reasonably, retain what is usable and adopt a method suited to the question, rather than treating the model as a compulsory ritual.","The twelve entry points, in order, are representational boundaries; Nash equilibrium and coordination; repetition and reputation; signaling and screening in Spence and others; incomplete information in Harsanyi and others; Schelling's commitment and deterrence; public choice in Olson and Buchanan; mechanism design and implementation in Hurwicz, Maskin, and Myerson; cooperative allocation; evolution and learning; networks and multilevel interaction; and conditions for leaving a game-theoretic explanation. The strongest objection says that if violations are possible in reality, a mechanism guarantee is meaningless. The response is that a guarantee within the original set may retain value, but cannot exceed its conditions. An extension requires renewed analysis, not a false claim that it has already been resolved."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Players","leftText":"Actors with decision nodes in the model.","rightTitle":"Represented and affected people","rightText":"The scope of rights and costs may be wider; the player list cannot remove them."},{"leftTitle":"Existence of equilibrium","leftText":"A combination exists with no profitable unilateral deviation under the specified conditions.","rightTitle":"Reaching it, efficiency, and justification","rightText":"Learning paths, overall outcomes, and normative permission require separate grounds."},{"leftTitle":"Costly signal","leftText":"Sending the signal incurs a cost.","rightTitle":"Discriminating signal","rightText":"Incentives across types, capacity to bear costs, and interpretation must also fit together."},{"leftTitle":"Truthful reporting","leftText":"At the message stage, no misreport is profitable within the contract.","rightTitle":"Reliable implementation","rightText":"Hidden actions, monitoring incentives, resources, and authority still need checking."},{"leftTitle":"Lawful action set","leftText":"The set of choices permitted by the rules.","rightTitle":"Actual action set","rightText":"May include violations in reality; including them in the model does not permit their execution."}],"boundaries":["A model does not confer authority to represent others. Third parties' rights and losses are not removed because they are not listed as players.","Equilibrium, dynamics, efficiency, and fairness do not vouch for one another; cost does not directly certify the sincerity of a signal.","The second-price teaching proof is limited to its explicit assumptions and unilateral message deviations; it does not cover collusion, refusal to pay, or disruption of implementation.","Deterrence, punishment, and related matters are analyzed only as concepts; no permission for real-world coercion or violations is granted.","The numbers and games are teaching constructions by the lesson author; no games with actual people or real transactions were conducted."],"questions":["Why does the existence of an equilibrium not guarantee high efficiency?","Why does the second-price proof establish weak dominance?","How should people not listed as players remain included?","Why must the problem be solved again after monitoring and resources are added?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-15/figures/section-d-15.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"An actual foundational theoretical text: Vickrey's argument for second-price bidding","href":"https://cramton.umd.edu/market-design-papers/vickrey-counterspeculation-auctions-and-competitive-sealed-tenders.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"An actual foundational theoretical text: Vickrey's argument for second-price bidding","text":"In Section III of his 1961 original, on printed pages 20–21, Vickrey proposes allocating to the highest bidder while charging the second-highest bid, explicitly excludes collusion among bidders, and compares the gains and losses from underbidding and overbidding. This lesson uses only that original theoretical passage to check the rule and its conditions; it does not describe it as an actual auction experiment or a solution to every implementation problem. The earlier numbers 10, 7, 11, and so on are separate constructions.","sourceLabel":"William Vickrey, Journal of Finance 16(1), 8–37, March 1961; scan of the original publication","sourceUrl":"https://cramton.umd.edu/market-design-papers/vickrey-counterspeculation-auctions-and-competitive-sealed-tenders.pdf","sourceLocator":"Printed pp20–21, §III, from the second paragraph beginning 'Since it has been shown…' through the end of the first paragraph on p21 ending 'unprofitable transaction'; PDF pages 14–15","boundary":"Limited to the specified theoretical passage; the full appendices, multi-item extensions, and actual implementation were not rechecked. Unilateral incentives at the reporting stage do not automatically cover violations, collusion, payment resources, or third-party losses.","href":"https://cramton.umd.edu/market-design-papers/vickrey-counterspeculation-auctions-and-competitive-sealed-tenders.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"D.15","start_line":4932,"end_line":4947,"startLine":4932,"endLine":4947}],"outputId":"section-d-15","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["List action nodes, information, actual choices, outcomes, and evaluation; separately include affected people who are not players.","Ask separately about equilibrium existence, selection, attainment, stability, efficiency, and justification; one result does not answer all six questions.","Check errors in repeated interaction, type-dependent signaling incentives, and future reasons and capabilities for fulfilling commitments.","Distinguish reporting from hidden execution, monitoring, funding, and third-party harms; identify lawful and actual action sets.","Solve again after adding new nodes or resources, retaining the original guarantee within its original scope. Include actual violations in e(t) without authorizing them."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: two equilibria with different efficiency","text":"Two players each choose A or B. Choosing A together yields (4,4), choosing B together yields (2,2), and choosing differently yields (0,0). AA and BB are both pure-strategy Nash equilibria because either player's unilateral switch lowers their payoff to 0; however, AA Pareto-dominates BB. The existence of an equilibrium does not resolve which one is selected, how it is reached, or the grounds for fairness.","assumptions":"Complete information and simultaneous actions; payoffs are determined solely by the table.","boundary":"This is not a measure of actual organizational welfare, and no necessarily convergent dynamics are proposed."},{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: the limited truthfulness guarantee of the second-price rule","text":"Suppose one's value is 10 and the highest bid from others is 7. A truthful bid of 10 wins, pays 7, and yields 3. Bidding 6 loses and yields 0; bidding 12 still wins, pays 7, and yields 3. If the others' highest bid is 11, truthful bidding loses and yields 0, while overbidding at 12 wins but yields −1. The general proof considers the three cases for v and m in the main explanation; it establishes weak dominance, not a strictly unique optimum every time.","assumptions":"A single item, private values, quasilinear payoffs, no collusion, ability to pay, and enforcement of the rules; other bids are fixed.","boundary":"No bidding advice for an actual auction is given, and not every mechanism objective or third party's welfare is guaranteed."},{"title":"Developing the source's teaching example: effective reporting, failed monitoring","text":"Suppose a demand-reporting rule achieves truthful reporting within its specified message set, but supervisors benefit personally from tolerating violations and face no effective checks. The demand reports can retain their limited guarantee while reliable implementation remains unsupported. Revision must include supervisors' actions, payoffs, authority, and resources, rather than treating truthful forms as completion of the entire chain.","assumptions":"A purely logical setup; no real-world cheating is designed and no evasion methods are provided.","boundary":"This does not teach specific violations. A truthful-reporting guarantee must be established for the actual mechanism, not asserted directly from a hypothetical example."},{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: equally costly signals can still fail to distinguish","text":"Suppose both types gain 6 units of benefit and pay 4 units of cost when signaling, while not signaling yields 0. Both types have a net benefit of 2 and therefore are willing to signal. The signal is costly but does not distinguish types in this model. If the difference in costs changes, incentives and recipients' interpretations must be checked again.","assumptions":"Benefits and costs are entirely constructed; no actual types are assessed.","boundary":"This does not classify people into morally superior and inferior types or infer actual goodwill."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"The mechanism makes people tell the truth, so supervisors will not violate the rules and third parties will not be harmed.","effect":"A message-stage guarantee does not cover hidden execution, monitoring incentives, or external harms.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"The rules prohibit an action, so it will not happen in reality and need not enter the model.","effect":"What is lawful differs from what is actually feasible. Possible violations can enter e(t) without obtaining permission for execution.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Both types paid a high cost, so the signal must distinguish their types.","effect":"The same net incentives can produce pooling; cost alone is insufficient.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why does the existence of an equilibrium not guarantee high efficiency?","answer":"In the coordination example, neither AA nor BB permits a profitable unilateral deviation, yet AA Pareto-dominates BB.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why does the second-price proof establish weak dominance?","answer":"Some deviations still win at the same price or still lose, so payoffs may be equal. A truthful bid need not be strictly unique.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"How should people not listed as players remain included?","answer":"Include them in the causal model if they have a control node; even without a node, retain evaluation of their costs and rights.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why must the problem be solved again after monitoring and resources are added?","answer":"Actions, payoffs, feasibility, and information structure change, so the old equilibrium and guarantees may not apply to the expanded model.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-15/figures/section-d-15.png","alt":"Specify actions, information, and choices first; players ≠ all affected people. Separate equilibrium existence and selection, attainment and stability, and efficiency and justification. For signals, check incentives across types; for commitments, check future reasons and actual ability to fulfill them. Truthful reporting offers a limited guarantee; hidden execution, monitoring, funding, and authority need separate checking. Lawful and actual action sets must not be conflated; including violations in a model does not authorize them. Solve again after adding nodes and resources; retain old guarantees within their original scope. An allocation criterion is not a uniquely fair allocation. Drawn by the lesson author from D.15; game values are purely for teaching. No auction bidding, punishment, or rule violation was carried out.","caption":"Drawn by the lesson author from D.15; game values are purely for teaching. No auction bidding, punishment, or rule violation was carried out."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not been implemented. Any selection, switching, or clicking described below is only a proposal for later design. Switching lawful and actual actions, supervisors' incentives, and resources would test whether the original guarantee still applies.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The original theory specifies a rule and a no-collusion condition, making it suitable for illustrating the scope of a guarantee. The teaching proof and the reading of the original text are identified separately; neither is presented as empirical research."},"openQuestions":["The complete original research for 15.01–15.12 was not obtained; no actual mechanism implementation or welfare assessment was conducted.","Only the specified theoretical passage in Vickrey was checked; the full appendices and multi-item extensions were not recalculated."],"taskId":"S-D-15","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical Chinese research draft, full-text lines 4753–4768; see sourceRefs for each section's precise range","claim_support":["D.15: terminology, contracts, argumentative responsibilities, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"vickrey1961","title":"Counterspeculation, Auctions, and Competitive Sealed Tenders","url":"https://cramton.umd.edu/market-design-papers/vickrey-counterspeculation-auctions-and-competitive-sealed-tenders.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"March1961，Journal of Finance16(1)，8—37","locator":"Printed pp20–21, §III, from the second paragraph through the end of the first paragraph on the following page (PDF pages 14–15)","claim_support":["The highest bidder pays the second-highest bid; the no-collusion condition and arguments about underbidding and overbidding"],"limits":["Limited to the single-item theoretical passage, not an actual experiment. The full appendices and implementation were not checked; the original guarantee cannot exceed its assumptions."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"D.15　博弈、合作、信号、承诺与实施; Chinese source lines 5206–5221; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-15/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-15/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-15/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-15/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"7ea04727d27a403f8a3dddeaf0a93cf64ee26790a9e364a80506748524e3fd99","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-15.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-15.md"}`),c={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-15.md"};function d(h,e,u,p,m,f){const t=a("SectionLesson");return n(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=s('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-d-15" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">D.15</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Games, Cooperation, Signals, Commitment, and Implementation <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-d-15" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D.15 Games, Cooperation, Signals, Commitment, and Implementation {#section-d-15}&quot;">​</a></h1><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">A rule that induces truthful reporting does not establish that implementers will execute it correctly or that people omitted from the model bear no costs. The value of game analysis depends on clearly stating the boundaries of actions, information, resources, and evaluation; the actions allowed by a model cannot be treated as all the possibilities in reality.</span></div><p>In the main text, see §10.5, §11.3 and 11.7, §12, and §14. Research entries cover 15.01–15.12.</p><p>A game representation starts by specifying actual decision nodes, information, feasible choices, outcomes, and evaluation. Being included among the players does not confer full authority to represent others. People outside the player set may still bear costs and have rights. A third party that can change permissions or resources should appear in the causal model. If it currently has no node at which it can exercise control, it still cannot be removed from the normative allocation.</p><p>Equilibrium existence, equilibrium selection, actual attainment, dynamic stability, efficiency, and normative justification each require their own grounds. Repeated interaction can sustain cooperation and can also undermine it through false alarms, short-term incentives, or uncontrolled punishment. A costly signal can distinguish types only if the recipient’s interpretation fits each type’s incentives to choose it and ability to bear its cost.</p><p>Distinguish the announcement of a commitment, reasons to honor it later, and the actual ability to honor it when the time comes. Deterrence differs from demanding reversal of an action already taken. Neither is shown to work by a forceful declaration alone. An assurance that the threat can end after compliance also matters. Truthful reporting in mechanism design does not automatically address hidden action, the incentives of overseers, multiple equilibria, or authority to implement.</p><p>In a teaching example, a rule gets members to report their needs truthfully but leaves oversight to people who benefit from violations. Reporting can work while implementation still fails. Revise the particular actions and chain of incentives. A general claim that rational models are useless does not identify what needs changing. The core, Shapley allocations, and bargaining solutions answer different questions about cooperation. They do not by themselves produce a unique fair allocation. An actual violation enters e(t). Simulating a violation does not confer permission to carry it out.</p><p>A guarantee about a mechanism must state the set of permitted actions. Actions permitted by the rules may differ from actions that can actually be taken. An unprofitable deviation in the former set does not show that violations in the latter are no longer attractive. A guarantee of truthful reporting also does not automatically cover hidden implementation, shared funding, or losses to third parties. After adding implementers and resource constraints, solve the corresponding mechanism again and retain the original guarantee within the scope of its original conditions.</p><p>Research topics. 15.01 Limits of game representations. 15.02 Nash equilibrium and coordination dilemmas. 15.03 Repeated games, reputation, and cooperation. 15.04 Signaling, screening, and cheap talk (Spence and others). 15.05 Incomplete information, beliefs, and chains of suspicion (Harsanyi and others). 15.06 Commitment, deterrence, and bargaining (Schelling and others). 15.07 Public choice, free riding, and collective action (Olson and Buchanan). 15.08 Mechanism design and implementation (Hurwicz, Maskin, and Myerson). 15.09 Cooperative games, coalitions, and allocation. 15.10 Evolutionary games and learning dynamics. 15.11 Network games and interaction across levels. 15.12 Conditions for stopping a game-theoretic explanation.</p></div>',1)),r(t,{section:"D.15"}),e[1]||(e[1]=l("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const b=i(c,[["render",d]]);export{y as __pageData,b as default};
